The crucial fracture is that there are several different kinds of “nothing”:

\varnothing_{\text{matter}}
\neq
\varnothing_{\text{pattern}}
\neq
\varnothing_{\text{category}}
\neq
\varnothing_{\text{address}}
\neq
\varnothing_{\text{function}}

Something can physically exist while being functionally nonexistent to a system because the system possesses no detector, distinction, category, or operator capable of addressing it.

That gives the novelty engine a stronger target:

\boxed{
\text{Novelty}
=
\text{make previously unaddressable structure operational}
}

And the really promising search direction is operator-induced ontology expansion:

\text{new operator}
\rightarrow
\text{new distinctions}
\rightarrow
\text{new objects}
\rightarrow
\text{new categories}
\rightarrow
\text{new compositions}
\rightarrow
\text{new operators}

So rather than only generating candidates, it searches for transformations that increase the generator’s future expressive/search capacity.

I’ve set the novelty engine to check daily and only alert you when something clears the paydirt threshold: a genuinely useful new capability, meaningful search-space expansion, or a reusable new Seal that survives the Fitness Route and validation checks.

That’s much better than asking it to manufacture “novel” things continuously. Paydirt is the stopping condition. ⛏️